When session tokens leak, every one of them is a working login until it expires. Maybe a logging tool captured cookies it shouldn't have. Maybe a dependency in your frontend was compromised. Maybe an admin account was taken over and you can't tell which users it touched.

Until now, the way to end those sessions was a script that walked every user through the Users API and deleted their sessions one by one. On a large project that takes a long time, and every minute it runs is a minute stolen sessions still work.

Today, we are announcing **force sign out** in Appwrite Auth.

# What force sign out gives you

- **One action for every user.** A single click in the Console or one API call signs out every user in the project.
- **Instant, at any scale.** Appwrite records one cutoff time on the project, so every older session stops working immediately, whether you have ten users or ten million.
- **Every client, every session.** Web, mobile, server SDKs, and JWTs created from those sessions are all cut off, and open realtime connections are closed.
- **Users get straight back in.** Nobody is locked out. Users sign in again as usual and get a fresh session.
- **Your servers keep running.** API keys are not affected.

# How force sign out works

Signing everyone out stores one timestamp on your project. On every request, Appwrite compares that timestamp with the time the session was created. A session created before it is treated as signed out, and the request runs as a guest.

The old session records are then deleted in the background. You don't have to wait for that cleanup, because the cutoff already applies to every request.

# Sign out all users from the Console

Open your project in the Appwrite Console and go to **Auth** > **Settings**. The **Security actions** card holds the project-wide actions for incidents: **Force sign out** and **Force password reset**.

{% only_dark %}
![The Security actions card in the Appwrite Console with the Force sign out and Force password reset actions](/images/blog/announcing-force-sign-out/security-actions-dark.avif)
{% /only_dark %}

{% only_light %}
![The Security actions card in the Appwrite Console with the Force sign out and Force password reset actions](/images/blog/announcing-force-sign-out/security-actions.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Click **Sign out all users** and confirm.

# Sign out all users from your server

The same action is available as `deleteSessions` on the Project service. Use an API key with the `users.write` scope:

```server-nodejs
import { Client, Project } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>');

const project = new Project(client);

await project.deleteSessions();
```

The action is recorded in your project's audit logs, so you keep a record of when it happened and which key or account triggered it.

# Handle the sign out in your app

After a force sign out, a signed-out user's next request runs as a guest, so `account.get()` fails with a `401`. Most apps already send users to the sign-in screen when that happens. If yours doesn't, check for it when your app loads:

```client-web
import { Client, Account } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

const account = new Account(client);

try {
    const user = await account.get();
} catch (error) {
    if (error.code === 401) {
        // The session is gone, show the sign-in screen
    }
}
```

Anonymous sessions can't be recovered after a force sign out, so those users start over with a new anonymous account.

# When to force a sign out

Sign out every user when you have reason to believe sessions are in someone else's hands:

- **Leaked session tokens or JWTs**, from logs, an error tracker, or a compromised frontend dependency.
- **A compromised admin account or API key** that could have created sessions for your users.

If passwords may have leaked as well, pair it with [force password reset](/blog/post/announcing-force-password-reset). Otherwise, users whose passwords were stolen are signed out only until the attacker signs back in.

For a single compromised account, you don't need a project-wide sign out. Delete that user's sessions with the Users API, or block the user until you've investigated.

# Getting started with force sign out in Appwrite Auth

Force sign out is live on [Appwrite Cloud](/sign-up) and in self-hosted Appwrite 2.7 and later.

